  "resolution_criteria": "This question resolves as the number of additional US states which enact a law prohibiting the sale of cultivated meat before July 1, 2027.\n\nThe following states will not be considered for this question:\n\n* Alabama\n* Florida&#x20;\n* Indiana\n* Mississippi\n* Montana\n* Nebraska\n* South Dakota\n* Texas&#x20;\n\nIf no new prohibitions on the sale of cultivated meat become law in any additional US state before July 1, 2027, this question resolves as **Zero**.",
  "fine_print": "This question will resolve based upon a ban being signed into law, rather than when it is implemented.&#x20;\n\n\"Cultivated meat\" is [defined](https://gfi.org/science/the-science-of-cultivated-meat/) as genuine animal meat, including seafood and organs, produced by the cultivation of cells in bioreactors.&#x20;\n\nFor it to count, a law must fully prohibit the sale of cultivated meat within the state. Therefore laws such as Iowa's [Senate File 2391](https://www.thegazette.com/news/state/capitol-notebook-iowa-house-passes-bill-to-ban-mislabeling-alternative-meat-egg-products/article_b2d84289-99b6-5939-8399-845ce9b2423b.html), which regulates and restricts cultivated meat but does not ban the sale of it, would not count.&#x20;\n\nAn enacted law sunsetting (as in [Indiana](https://www.theindianalawyer.com/articles/trenton-morton-scrutinizing-indianas-rules-for-cultured-meat-labeling)) or being later overturned or enjoined (as was [attempted](https://ij.org/press-release/federal-judge-denies-preliminary-injunction-in-upside-foods-lawsuit-against-floridas-ban-on-cultivated-meat/) in Florida) will still count if it results in the prohibition of the sale of cultivated meat.\n\nPartial bans (for example laws prohibiting the sale of cultivated meat to persons under 21 years of age) will not count.&#x20;\n\nThis question pertains to the sale of cultivated meat within a state, not to the purchase of it by individuals.\n\nPlease note that a trigger law (defined [here](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/trigger_laws)) only counts if the specified shift in controlling law occurs. An example of this is Louisiana SB 152, which [says](https://www.legis.la.gov/Legis/ViewDocument.aspx?d=1480467):&#x20;\n\n> The provisions of this Part shall become effective only upon a final ruling by the United States Supreme Court holding that prohibitions on cell cultured food products do not violate the United States Constitution",
  "background": "Cultivated or cultured meat is living meat cells grown in a laboratory rather than from the flesh of a living animal. The idea has gained support due to its potential to reduce animal suffering globally. Consumer attitudes have been mixed, with 45% of Americans saying they were \"likely to try\" cultivated meat and 44% unlikely to try it, according to a survey by the Good Food Institute (see page 10 [here](https://gfi.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Explaining-cultivated-meat-to-U.S.-consumers.pdf)).&#x20;\n\nTheoretically, cultivated meat has the potential to match animal-derived meat in taste, texture and nutritional composition. However, according to the *Food Bioscience* paper [Sensorial and Nutritional Aspects of Cultured Meat in Comparison to Traditional Meat: Much to Be Inferred](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/nutrition/articles/10.3389/fnut.2020.00035/), a vast amount of research & development remains to be done in the sector, in every major area:\n\n> Based on the available state of the art regarding production processes, it can be inferred that cultured meat currently differs significantly from traditional meat in its technological, sensorial and nutritional properties. Revealing the extent to which post-mortem processes occur in cultured meat is crucial to understand its impact on sensorial and technological properties. Production of cultured meat resembling fresh, unprocessed meat entails the biggest challenges with respect to texture, color, flavor as well as nutritional composition. Ideally, this would entail co-culturing of myoblasts with fibroblasts and adipocytes. In addition, electrical and/or mechanical stimulation may be needed to improve the techno-functional quality of the meat proteins. However, the technological and economic feasibility of these solutions, especially at large scale, can be questioned. With regard to nutritional value, we illustrated the long trajectory of additional research that is needed before the composition of cultured meat could resemble traditional meat, as well as the complexity of the medium composition needed to achieve this. This will not only add to the cost of the medium, but also increase the environmental footprint of the entire process. In processed meat products, most of the challenges mentioned above may be overcome by the simple addition of texturizing ingredients, colorants, flavorings and nutrients in order to remedy the sensorial and nutritional properties. However, this decreases consumer acceptability.\n\nAs of April 2026, the following states had enacted bans on the sales of cultivated meat:&#x20;\n\n[Alabama](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2024/05/13/lab-grown-meat-ban-alabama/73678952007/), [Florida](https://www.cbsnews.com/miami/news/federal-appeals-court-upholds-floridas-ban-on-lab-grown-meat-protecting-states-real-food-law/), [Indiana](https://www.theindianalawyer.com/articles/trenton-morton-scrutinizing-indianas-rules-for-cultured-meat-labeling), [Mississippi](https://magnoliatribune.com/2025/04/08/mississippi-bans-production-sale-of-lab-grown-meat/), [Montana](https://thebrooksinstitute.org/animal-law-digest/us/issue-293/montana-bans-sale-cultivated-meat), [Nebraska](https://nebraskaexaminer.com/2025/07/17/gov-jim-pillen-celebrates-nebraska-ban-on-lab-grown-meat-at-oneill-butcher-shop/), [South Dakota](https://www.brownfieldagnews.com/news/south-dakota-enacts-5-year-ban-on-cell-cultured-protein/), and [Texas](https://www.texaspolicyresearch.com/texas-cultivated-meat-ban-under-constitutional-scrutiny/).\n\nOn the federal level, cultured chicken cells from Upside Foods and Good Meat [received](https://apnews.com/article/cultivated-meat-lab-grown-cell-based-a88ab8e0241712b501aa191cdbf6b39a) regulatory approval in 2023 to be sold in the US, although as of 2026 neither company had begun sales to end consumers.&#x20;\n\nAt the time of this question, the following state legislatures have recently considered bans:\n\n* [Georgia](https://senatepress.net/sen-jason-t-dickerson-introduces-legislation-to-ban-lab-grown-meat.html)&#x20;\n* [Maine](https://legiscan.com/ME/bill/LD1257/2025)\n* [Oklahoma](https://www.brownfieldagnews.com/news/bill-to-ban-lab-grown-protein-gains-momentum-in-oklahoma-senate/)\n* [South Carolina](https://www.billtrack50.com/billdetail/1963536)\n* [West Virginia](https://www.wboy.com/news/west-virginia/west-virginia-politics/state-legislature-advances-bill-banning-lab-grown-meat/)",
  "nr_forecasters": 51,
